{
  "image_name": "cd6f5a918a956f6524732ce859ea6991.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how a semiconductor photocatalyst and a molecular photocatalyst (MPC) handle light-driven redox chemistry, and relates both to reaction energetics. In the semiconductor sketch, light creates separated negative and positive charges at opposite regions of the material, which then reduce an acceptor and oxidize a donor at the surface. The MPC scheme shows the same overall function but as a cyclic sequence of discrete molecular states: light excites the catalyst to an energized state, after which electron transfer steps generate reduced and oxidized forms that react with an acceptor and donor before returning to the ground-state catalyst.\n\nThe energy diagram on the right summarizes why an MPC can access different photochemical routes. A green biomass-derived substrate starts at relatively low energy, blue intermediates lie higher, and the orange product ends lower than the starting point. Three routes are shown with increasing energetic reach. Route I appears unable to cross a substantial barrier without photoexcitation. Additional photon energy from the photocatalyst raises the system to higher-energy intermediates in Routes II and III, enabling access to reaction pathways that then descend to product. Overall, the figure emphasizes that molecular photocatalysts can store light energy in well-defined redox states and open otherwise inaccessible biomass conversion pathways.",
